      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I understand you need a clear, medical explanation for your patient without the goblin analogies. I apologize for the previous deviation; I will focus strictly on the physiological mechanisms of insulin, glycemic index, and blood sugar regulation.\n\nHigh-glycemic index (GI) foods are typically simple carbohydrates, such as white bread, sugary drinks, or ripe bananas, that are rapidly digested and absorbed in the small intestine. When these carbohydrates enter the bloodstream, they cause a sharp and immediate increase in blood glucose levels. The pancreas detects this rapid spike and responds by secreting a large, sudden burst of insulin. This insulin acts like a key, opening the doors on muscle cells and fat cells to allow glucose to rush inside for energy or storage. However, because the initial glucose load was so high, the insulin surge often overshoots, leading to a temporary drop in blood sugar (reactive hypoglycemia) shortly after the meal, which can cause fatigue or hunger.\n\nLow-glycemic index (GI) foods, such as whole grains, legumes, and most non-starchy vegetables, consist of complex carbohydrates with a higher fiber content. These foods digest much more slowly because the fiber forms a gel",
      "source": "target",
